    "content_html": "<div class=\"articlebodycontent col-xl-9 col-lg-12 col-md-12 col-sm-12 col-12\" id=\"content-body-71671270\"><div class=\"schemaDiv\" id=\"schemaDiv\"><p><span class=\"drop-caps\">S</span>ome cities can simply be seen. Istanbul has to be walked into, and then walked through, and even then it keeps something back. Within a couple of hours of landing I had the particular dizziness of standing in a place that refuses to belong to any single century, and it never quite wore off.</p><p>We came as a family for nine days, Istanbul first, then the volcanic valleys of Cappadocia, then Antalya, where the old town tips straight down into impossibly blue Mediterranean water. It was Istanbul that stayed. Roman foundations, Byzantine domes, Ottoman minarets, and then, without warning, a glass-fronted cafe with a tram gliding past the window. The city does not bury its past to build its present. It lets the past remain, stitched into every seam.</p></div><div class=\"articleblock-container\" id=\"arthardpv\"></div><div class=\"update-publish-time article-published-time-end\"><p class=\"publish-time-new\"> Published <span>- November 08, 2026 05:05 am IST</span></p></div><div class=\"comments-shares share-page flex print-hide\" data-artid=\"71671270\" data-title=\"Lost in Istanbul\" data-url=\"https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/open-page/lost-in-istanbul/article71671270.ece\"><div class=\"left\"><div class=\"share\"><button class=\"btn share-btn\" data-ga-labelclick=\"ArticleShare\"></button><ul class=\"share-list bottom\" style=\"display: none;\"><li class=\"copy-link\" data-cf-modified-9304729ea03f5f4f944fbad8-=\"\" onclick=\"if (!window.__cfRLUnblockHandlers) return false; copytext('https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/open-page/lost-in-istanbul/article71671270.ece');\">\n                Copy link  \n                        \n            </li><li class=\"email\"><a href=\"mailto:?subject=Lost%20in%20Istanbul&amp;amp;body=Check%20out%20this%20link%20https%3A%2F%2Fww